trustees directed its advisor to decrease the percentage of
capital invested by United Mortgage Trust in long-term loans and
to increase the capital invested in interim loans. This decision
by United Mortgage Trust&#146;s trustees was driven by the
higher yield, lower default rates and lower loan-to-value ratio
that had been realized by United Mortgage Trust in its previous
interim loans. The long-term loan portfolio was reduced by
United Mortgage Trust through prepayment by borrowers, sales of
loans to third-party investors and sales of loans through
securitization.
