<DIV align="left" style="font-size: 10pt; margin-top: 6pt; margin-left: 0; margin-right: 0; margin-bottom: 0; color: #000000; background: #ffffff;">&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
As noted under &#147;&#151;&nbsp;General Principles of
Partnership Taxation&#148; above, partnerships are not liable
for income taxes imposed by the Internal Revenue Code. The
Treasury Regulations set forth broad &#147;anti-abuse&#148;
rules applicable to partnerships, however, which rules authorize
the Commissioner of the Internal Revenue Service to recast
transactions involving the use of partnerships either to reflect
the underlying economic arrangement or to prevent the use of a
partnership to circumvent the intended purpose of any provision
of the Internal Revenue Code. Our general partner is not aware
of any fact or circumstance that could cause the Commissioner to
exercise his authority under these rules; however, if any of the
transactions entered into by us were to be recharacterized under
these rules, or if we were to be recast as a taxable entity
under these rules, material adverse tax consequences to all of
our partners would occur as otherwise described in this
prospectus.
</DIV>

